#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --quiet --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = ["agent-client-protocol>=0.9,<1.0"]  # 1.0 (rc) not tested yet
# ///
"""Run one prompt through a coding agent over the Agent Client Protocol (ACP).

    acp-run <agent> [-C DIR] [--add-dir DIR]... [-p PROMPT | -f FILE | stdin] [--perm POLICY]
            [--keep-user-config] [--strip-user-config] [--model M] [--mode M] [--set ID=VALUE]... [--timeout SECS]
            [--effort LEVEL] [--context SIZE] [--meta JSON] [--agent-arg ARG]... [--agent-env K=V]...
            [--log PATH] [--json] [--handshake-tries N] [--handshake-timeout S]
            [--no-fallback] [--prompt-retries N] [--max-load L] [--load-wait S]
            [--wrapup-grace S [--wrapup TEXT]]
            [--verify CMD [--verify-retries N] [--verify-timeout S]]
            [-i | --interactive [--inbox DIR] [--show-thinking]] [--resume ID]
            [--session-id UUID]
    acp-run --list            agents and the command each one runs
    acp-run <agent> --info    initialize + session/new only; print modes,
                              config options (models), auth methods and
                              capabilities (loadSession, sessionCapabilities)
    acp-run <agent> --info --model M [--effort L] [--context S] [--set ..]
                              apply those first (no prompt) and show the options
                              that result; --all-models does it for every model

The agent's final message goes to stdout. A summary line goes to stderr
(stop reason, seconds, tool calls, permissions, tokens, cost, log path). Every
ACP event is logged as JSONL (including the prompt response) and ends with a
"result" record holding the summary and exit code; the default log is under
~/.local/state/acp-run/. --json prints the summary fields plus "text", and
"failure", "warning" and "quota" (an agent's _meta.quota) when present.

--add-dir DIR adds an extra workspace root for this session (repeatable).
The absolute paths are sent as additionalDirectories in session/new, allowing
the agent's file tools to access paths outside -C (e.g. bigteam report folders).

--perm decides each session/request_permission the agent sends:
    all              allow everything (default; also under --interactive)
    deny             refuse everything (no tools at all; a pure-text answer)
    read-only        the agent may read (tool kinds read, search, fetch) and nothing else:
                     what an advisory review needs (claude, codex, opencode; every
                     other agent is refused with exit 2); any other tool call trips the
                     tripwire (exit 77)
acp-run runs yolo for every agent (operator 2026-10-14): there is no other
permission-gating policy. The old --perm scoped:PATHS and --perm ask are still
accepted but deprecated: they print one warning on stderr and run as --perm all.
The answer is always an offered allow_once or reject_once option, never an
*_always one (those can be saved beyond this run) and never "cancelled" (that
cancels the whole turn). An allow with no allow_once offered becomes a
reject_once; with neither offered the agent gets a JSON-RPC error.
Under --perm deny (without --interactive) opencode and grok start without
the user's plugins, MCP servers and hooks, as claude does: a per-run temp
config (opencode: OPENCODE_CONFIG_DIR holding only provider/model keys, project
config off; grok: GROK_HOME without hooks/,
skills/, plugins/, whose config.toml has no hooks, plugins or MCP servers and asks
before every tool), removed afterwards and logged as a "user_config" record. Under
--perm deny grok also starts without --always-approve (it is the only way grok
sends a permission request; before 2026-10-15 every grok deny run tripped, exit 77). --keep-user-config (env ACP_RUN_KEEP_USER_CONFIG=1)
keeps them. (agy, crush and zcode had the same strip until they retired, 2026-11-06.)
Under every other non-interactive --perm grok also starts on that stripped GROK_HOME (operator
2026-10-15), but only hooks/, skills/, plugins/ and the config's hooks and plugins go: MCP servers and the
permission mode stay. ACP_RUN_GROK_USER_HOOKS=1 (or --keep-user-config) keeps the user's ~/.grok.
The "user_config" record names the "home" used.
--strip-user-config (env ACP_RUN_STRIP_USER_CONFIG=1) applies the same strip of
the user's plugins, MCP servers and hooks under ANY --perm (not --interactive),
for opencode and grok; it is a startup-reliability switch, not a
permission control (operator 2026-10-13: the SkillOpt-Sleep night runs --perm all
with it). Outside --perm deny nothing else changes: opencode gets no deny
permission. Under
--perm deny it changes nothing (deny already strips). Temp dirs are named
acp-run-<agent>-strip-<pid>-*; --keep-user-config still wins.
--set applies any config option --info lists (e.g. --set thinking=high).
--effort LEVEL (none, minimal, low, medium, high, xhigh, max, ultra, default; extra-high is
xhigh) and --context SIZE (200k, 256k, 272k, 400k, 500k, 1m or a token count) are normalized
across agents. Each is translated to the agent's own knob (an ACP option, a launch flag or
env var, grok's session/set_model, claude's CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_1M_CONTEXT,
...) and applied after the model and every other option, since a model switch resets
them. One the agent or model cannot take (hermes effort; opencode/
hermes context; a size the model does not list; a codex size above the model's
max_context_window) exits 2 before any prompt, naming the valid values. After the options are set and
after the turn (usage_update.size) acp-run reads back what the agent reports and prints
"acp-run: applied: model=... effort=... ctx=..." (values it cannot read back are marked
(unverified)); the same goes into the "result" record and --json as "applied". A requested
value the agent reports differently is failure class "config", exit 1. --meta JSON is merged
into session/new _meta. --agent-arg ARG / --agent-env K=V append to the agent's launch
command / environment (per host: ACP_RUN_ARGS_<AGENT> split like a shell line,
ACP_RUN_ENV_<AGENT>="K=V,K=V").
The agent never sees HERDR_PANE_ID, HERDR_ENV, HERDR_WORKSPACE_ID, HERDR_TAB_ID, PASEO_AGENT_ID, PASEO_AGENT_CWD or
ORCA_TERMINAL_HANDLE (acp-run drops them on every run, not only --interactive: an agent
hook once renamed the caller's herdr tab; a slice that kept PASEO_AGENT_ID could clear or
archive the caller's Paseo agent, 2026-10-20) and always sees ACP_RUN_AGENT=1.
Per-host settings: ~/.config/acp-run.env (or $ACP_RUN_ENV_FILE), when present,
holds KEY=VALUE / export KEY="VALUE" lines (no shell expansion) loaded into the
environment unless already set, e.g. ACP_RUN_DEFAULT_SET_CLAUDE="model=sonnet,effort=high"
(merged over that agent's defaults; --model/--set still win).
cursor: plain model ids plus context/reasoning_effort/fast options (model applied
first), e.g. acp-run cursor --model grok-4.7 --context 500k --set fast=false
--effort high; bracketed preset strings no longer apply.
Some agents may get defaults (DEFAULT_SET below; none since cline retired, 2026-11-06).
Agents decide what to ask about: some auto-allow by their own settings (use
--mode to pick a stricter one; --info lists the modes).

Delivery checks: a turn that ends with end_turn is still a failure (exit 1,
with "FAILED <class>" on stderr) when the agent returned a provider refusal as
its answer, its reply ends in a refusal paragraph (an agent may append "Usage Limit
Reached ..." to the progress text it was streaming; a 429 in the agent's stderr
during the turn also makes a quota mention in that last paragraph one), or its
reply ends in a provider/transport error line, or the whole short reply is the
model declining the task ("Sorry, I cannot fulfill your request ..."). The patterns
are the FAILURE_CLASSES, REPLY_REFUSALS, MODEL_REFUSALS and TAIL_ERROR tables in this script:
    refused    the model declined the task (never retried; not a deliverable)
    quota      usage/rate limit or quota exhausted
    plan       model not available on the account's plan
    auth       not logged in / authentication required
    transient  timeout, unavailable, overloaded, connection reset, 5xx
A reply cut off by such an error line is reported as "reply truncated; edits
may be present". ACP errors are classified the same way.

--prompt-retries N retries the prompt up to N times (backoff 1, 2, 4.. 10 s)
when it failed with a transient error before any tool call or permission
request; quota, plan and auth failures are never retried. Default 0.
--max-load L (env ACP_RUN_MAX_LOAD) refuses to start while the 1-minute load
average per CPU core is above L; with --load-wait S (env ACP_RUN_LOAD_WAIT)
it waits up to S seconds for the load to drop first. Off by default.
--handshake-timeout S (env ACP_RUN_HANDSHAKE_TIMEOUT) bounds initialize and
session/new each; default 40 s, claude 90 s (HANDSHAKE_TIMEOUT).
--handshake-tries N (default 2) starts a fresh agent after a handshake timeout,
or after a transient ACP error in initialize or session/new (backoff 1, 2, 4..
10 s; nothing has run yet). The error names the step that hung.
--wrapup-grace S: when --timeout cancels the turn, send the agent one more
prompt (--wrapup TEXT, default "time is up, write your final report now") and
wait up to S seconds for it before stopping, so a slice cut off mid-command can
still write its report. The run still exits 124; the summary line says
wrapup=<stop reason|timeout|skipped|error>. Off by default; acp-dispatch turns
it on. Not with --interactive.
--verify CMD runs a shell command (with -C DIR as cwd; exit 0 = pass) after a
turn ends with end_turn and passes delivery checks. If the command fails,
acp-run sends a follow-up prompt into the same session with the exit code and
the last output (up to 40 lines, max 3000 chars) instructing the agent to fix
the problem and retry, repeated up to --verify-retries N times (default 2).
--verify-timeout SECS sets the timeout for each verify command execution
(default 180). Respects the overall --timeout deadline; a final failed
verification logs verify=fail and prints a warning on stderr without failing
the exit code. Off by default. Not with --interactive.

--interactive (-i) keeps the session open for more prompts in one process (the
ACP connection is the session). Agent text streams to stdout as it arrives,
each tool call is one "⚙ <title>" line, a failed tool call one "✗" line, and
thoughts only with --show-thinking. After each turn it prints the stop reason
and an idle line, then reads the next prompt from whichever comes first: a
line on stdin (one line, one prompt) or a *.txt/*.md file dropped into
--inbox DIR (polled every second, taken in name order, deleted once read; the
whole file is one prompt). The first prompt comes from -p or -f only; without
either it starts at the prompt. Commands: /exit (or EOF) ends the session;
/exit typed at the terminal during a turn cancels it first, while from a pipe
or the inbox it waits behind the prompts before it. /cancel cancels the running
turn. Ctrl-C cancels the running turn, or exits when idle. Prompts that arrive
during a turn queue up as the next ones. --timeout applies to each turn. Each
turn gets the delivery checks; a failed one prints "FAILED <class>" and returns
to the prompt. On exit: the stderr summary line (turns=N), or --json with the
last turn's text; the exit code is the last turn's (0 when no turn ran).
In a herdr pane (HERDR_PANE_ID set, HERDR_BIN_PATH or herdr found) it reports
state working/idle as source acp-run with a resume command
(acp-run <agent> -C DIR --interactive --resume ID ...; dropped on herdr before
0.9.2, which rejects it), and releases the pane on exit, so `herdr agent prompt
<pane> TEXT` and `herdr agent list` work. The agent process gets no
HERDR_PANE_ID/HERDR_ENV, so its own herdr hooks do not report the pane too.
--resume ID reopens an ACP session with session/resume (no replay) or, when
the agent has no resume or it fails with -32601/-32602, session/load (history
replayed to the log only); exit 2 if it advertises neither, or if the agent has
no such session. session/close ends every run when advertised.
--session-id UUID (claude): the conversation id the claude CLI runs as when
acp-run falls back to it after an ACP handshake hang (`claude -p
--session-id`); without it the fallback picks a fresh UUID. Either way the
log gets a new_session record with that id (via "claude-cli"), so a later
--resume lands in exactly that conversation. Over ACP claude-agent-acp picks
the id itself and hands it to Claude as its --session-id, so the new_session
record is already Claude's own id.

Exit codes: 0 end_turn with some output; 1 other stop reason, agent error,
an empty reply (no text, no tool calls; or no text under --perm deny or read-only
or after a denied permission), a delivery-check failure (quota/plan/auth/provider
error, truncated reply, or FAILED fake-tool-calls: a reply of tool calls printed as
text when no tool ran) or a requested model/effort/context the agent did
not apply (FAILED config); 2 usage error (including an --effort/--context the
agent or model cannot take); 5 empty reply and every permission
request was denied; 75 refused by --max-load; 127 the agent binary is not on PATH (after
waiting ACP_RUN_BIN_WAIT seconds, default 90, 0 disables, at most 600, for an upgrade to relink it); 77 under --perm deny or read-only a tool ran
without a permission request (tripwire; turn cancelled); 124 timed out (session/cancel
sent first, then the process is killed); 130/143 interrupted by SIGINT/SIGTERM
(session/cancel sent, agent stopped); with --interactive also 129 on SIGHUP,
and Ctrl-C (SIGINT) does not exit while a turn runs.
"""

